metric space with this metric.

240
00:21:56,290 --> 00:21:59,060
Now, why is this the case?

241
00:21:59,060 --> 00:22:01,420
So this is just that--

242
00:22:01,420 --> 00:22:07,540
so proof of that is really just
a one-liner because you see that

243
00:22:07,540 --> 00:22:08,620
to say that--

244
00:22:11,620 --> 00:22:18,700
so that f of x minus
fn of x is less than

245
00:22:18,700 --> 00:22:34,450
or equal to epsilon for all x
if this here is the case, where

246
00:22:34,450 --> 00:22:42,250
this is equivalent to saying
that the distance between fn

247
00:22:42,250 --> 00:22:48,270
and an f is less than or
equal to epsilon for all x.

248
00:22:51,930 --> 00:22:54,850
Because if this is
the case for all x,

249
00:22:54,850 --> 00:22:57,830
then the maximum is less
than or equal to epsilon.

250
00:22:57,830 --> 00:22:59,410
This was the maximum.

251
00:22:59,410 --> 00:23:02,090
And on the other hand, if
the maximum, well, then it

252
00:23:02,090 --> 00:23:04,090
holds for each x.

253
00:23:04,090 --> 00:23:06,130
So this is sort of a tautology.

254
00:23:08,910 --> 00:23:14,250
And so now we see, of course,
that if the sequence-- so you

255
00:23:14,250 --> 00:23:15,730
have that.

256
00:23:15,730 --> 00:23:23,050
But this means-- so this
means that if the sequence--

257
00:23:23,050 --> 00:23:36,660
therefore, if the sequence fn
converges to f uniformly, well,

258
00:23:36,660 --> 00:23:39,760
then it means that
for all epsilon,

259
00:23:39,760 --> 00:23:44,040
if we're sufficiently far
out, then this here can be--

260
00:23:44,040 --> 00:23:47,180
for any given epsilon, if
you're sufficiently far out,

261
00:23:47,180 --> 00:23:49,828
this here can be made less
than or equal to epsilon, which

262
00:23:49,828 --> 00:23:51,620
means that this error
can be made less than

263
00:23:51,620 --> 00:23:54,720
or equal to epsilon if
you're sufficiently far out.

264
00:23:54,720 --> 00:23:57,600
So you see that if this
converges uniformly, then

265
00:23:57,600 --> 00:24:04,680
it implies that this
thing here converges to 0.

266
00:24:04,680 --> 00:24:06,800
But that's exactly
what it means,

267
00:24:06,800 --> 00:24:14,010
that this sequence fn converges
to f in the metric space.

268
00:24:23,040 --> 00:24:24,410
And the other way around.

269
00:24:27,280 --> 00:24:30,840
If this sequence
converges, fn converges

270
00:24:30,840 --> 00:24:33,000
to f in the metric
space, this is

271
00:24:33,000 --> 00:24:36,480
equivalent to saying
that this here goes to 0.

272
00:24:36,480 --> 00:24:39,560
But if this here
goes to 0, this means

273
00:24:39,560 --> 00:24:42,480
that if you're sufficiently
far out-- for a given

274
00:24:42,480 --> 00:24:44,360
epsilon, if you're
sufficiently far out,

275
00:24:44,360 --> 00:24:46,980
this here is less than
or equal to epsilon.

276
00:24:46,980 --> 00:24:49,400
But it means that this
here is less than epsilon.

277
00:24:49,400 --> 00:24:54,080
So that means it really
converges uniformly.

278
00:24:54,080 --> 00:24:56,445
OK.

279
00:24:56,445 --> 00:25:01,920
And that's in a way that
makes it much more natural.

280
00:25:01,920 --> 00:25:04,680
I mean, yeah.

281
00:25:04,680 --> 00:25:11,920
So it makes uniform
convergence much more natural.

282
00:25:11,920 --> 00:25:16,840
So now let's prove
the next term.

283
00:25:16,840 --> 00:25:21,040
So for this theorem,
we're going to think

284
00:25:21,040 --> 00:25:27,570
about uniform convergence
in terms of this metric

285
00:25:27,570 --> 00:25:31,510
and in terms of convergence
in this metric space.

286
00:25:31,510 --> 00:25:36,210
So this theorem
is the next thing.

287
00:25:36,210 --> 00:25:41,990
Let's say that if you take
an interval from a to b

288
00:25:41,990 --> 00:25:45,350
and you're looking at
the continuous function

289
00:25:45,350 --> 00:25:50,110
and you equip it with this
metric that we talked about,

290
00:25:50,110 --> 00:25:54,290
then this here is this space,
which is a metric space.

291
00:25:54,290 --> 00:26:03,300
So this metric space
is a Cauchy complete.

292
00:26:13,250 --> 00:26:16,390
So remember, Cauchy
complete in a metric space

293
00:26:16,390 --> 00:26:20,670
means that if you take a
Cauchy sequence-- so just let

294
00:26:20,670 --> 00:26:22,950
me just recall here over here.

295
00:26:22,950 --> 00:26:24,590
So Cauchy.

296
00:26:24,590 --> 00:26:34,070
So if x comma d
is a metric space,

297
00:26:34,070 --> 00:27:00,270
then Cauchy complete means
that any Cauchy sequence

298
00:27:00,270 --> 00:27:01,450
is convergent.

299
00:27:07,030 --> 00:27:10,490
That's what it means to
be a Cauchy complete.

300
00:27:10,490 --> 00:27:17,070
And of course, an example of
a Cauchy complete metric space

301
00:27:17,070 --> 00:27:21,610
was just if we took R
with the usual metric.

302
00:27:26,532 --> 00:27:29,240
So what we have here is--

303
00:27:29,240 --> 00:27:30,520
so let's try to prove this.

304
00:27:33,460 --> 00:27:35,000
So we need to prove.

305
00:27:35,000 --> 00:27:38,340
So this here is now--

306
00:27:38,340 --> 00:27:51,460
we need to prove that if this
here is a Cauchy sequence,

307
00:27:51,460 --> 00:28:04,566
then there exists a function
that is also continuous.

308
00:28:07,580 --> 00:28:22,050
And so this sequence here
converging to f in the metric d.

309
00:28:22,050 --> 00:28:23,930
That's what we need to prove.

310
00:28:23,930 --> 00:28:31,890
So in particular, we need to
first construct a function, f.

311
00:28:36,090 --> 00:28:40,320
So now let's try first
to construct a function.

312
00:28:47,890 --> 00:29:01,400
So we first construct
f as follows.

313
00:29:07,090 --> 00:29:11,570
Well, if you fix x--

314
00:29:11,570 --> 00:29:16,490
so x is something, a
point in the interval.

315
00:29:16,490 --> 00:29:20,590
And now look at
this sequence fn.

316
00:29:20,590 --> 00:29:24,490
This is now a regular sequence.

317
00:29:24,490 --> 00:29:28,010
But if you're looking
at the sequence,

318
00:29:28,010 --> 00:29:38,090
then we have that fn of x minus
fm of x, if you have n and m

319
00:29:38,090 --> 00:29:41,650
like this, then
the difference here

320
00:29:41,650 --> 00:29:47,530
is less than or equal
to the max where

321
00:29:47,530 --> 00:29:59,570
this is for all z in
the interval of fn

322
00:29:59,570 --> 00:30:11,386
of z minus fm of z, like that.

323
00:30:11,386 --> 00:30:17,520
Because of course, it's like
in particular if z was x.

324
00:30:17,520 --> 00:30:18,830
So you have clearly this.

325
00:30:21,720 --> 00:30:23,680
But now you see that--

326
00:30:23,680 --> 00:30:27,420
so I claim that this means--

327
00:30:27,420 --> 00:30:43,040
so I claim that this implies
that this sequence here--

328
00:30:43,040 --> 00:30:45,720
sorry, this sequence here.

329
00:30:45,720 --> 00:30:50,160
fn of x, this
sequence here, which

330
00:30:50,160 --> 00:30:53,000
is a sequence of real numbers--

331
00:30:53,000 --> 00:30:55,480
these here are real numbers--

332
00:30:55,480 --> 00:31:06,350
that this here is a Cauchy
sequence of real numbers.

333
00:31:13,120 --> 00:31:16,610
Why is that the case?

334
00:31:16,610 --> 00:31:24,250
Well, it's the case
because given--

335
00:31:24,250 --> 00:31:28,150
so this is just because given--

336
00:31:28,150 --> 00:31:43,070
so this is the case because
given epsilon greater than 0,

337
00:31:43,070 --> 00:31:56,300
since fn is a Cauchy sequence
in the metric space--

338
00:32:00,434 --> 00:32:02,710
in this metric space--

339
00:32:02,710 --> 00:32:14,590
then there exist capital N so
that if little n and little m

340
00:32:14,590 --> 00:32:17,910
are bigger than capital
N, then the difference

341
00:32:17,910 --> 00:32:19,530
between these guys--

342
00:32:19,530 --> 00:32:23,410
sorry, the difference
between this function here

343
00:32:23,410 --> 00:32:28,890
and that function is
less than epsilon.

344
00:32:28,890 --> 00:32:32,230
This is because they
were Cauchy sequence.

345
00:32:32,230 --> 00:32:35,450
And so now this thing
here is, of course--

346
00:32:35,450 --> 00:32:38,090
this thing here, that's
what we just saw.

347
00:32:38,090 --> 00:32:39,990
This is the maximum.

348
00:32:39,990 --> 00:32:48,950
So this is bigger than when you
evaluate it in just one point,

349
00:32:48,950 --> 00:32:51,390
like that.

350
00:32:51,390 --> 00:33:00,510
And so this proof
that when x is fixed,

351
00:33:00,510 --> 00:33:04,870
these guys here is a Cauchy
sequence of real numbers.

352
00:33:04,870 --> 00:33:06,270
So now we use--

353
00:33:06,270 --> 00:33:20,740
since R here is
Cauchy complete and fn

354
00:33:20,740 --> 00:33:29,340
of x is a Cauchy
sequence, then it

355
00:33:29,340 --> 00:33:37,340
means that the sequence fn of
x, that this is converging,

356
00:33:37,340 --> 00:33:42,860
and it's converging to some
number, to just one number.

357
00:33:42,860 --> 00:33:49,540
And that number we
can call f of x.

358
00:33:49,540 --> 00:33:55,420
So we just defined f to be
the limit of the sequence.

359
00:33:55,420 --> 00:33:58,220
And that's well defined because
there's just one number.

360
00:33:58,220 --> 00:34:02,140
So for each x it
gives you a number.

361
00:34:02,140 --> 00:34:03,020
OK.

362
00:34:03,020 --> 00:34:04,970
So now what we have proven is--

363
00:34:08,340 --> 00:34:11,139
so now we have a candidate.

364
00:34:11,139 --> 00:34:16,090
So namely, we have shown that--

365
00:34:16,090 --> 00:34:18,889
so now what we
have proven here is

366
00:34:18,889 --> 00:34:24,330
that we have found a function.

367
00:34:24,330 --> 00:34:29,610
So we have now proven
that this sequence here is

368
00:34:29,610 --> 00:34:32,889
converging to some function f.

369
00:34:32,889 --> 00:34:35,830
The convergence
is just pointwise.

370
00:34:39,409 --> 00:34:44,130
And we don't know whether this
function is continuous or not.

371
00:34:44,130 --> 00:34:45,670
The convergence is pointwise.

372
00:34:45,670 --> 00:34:52,130
This is just because
for each fixed x, f of x

373
00:34:52,130 --> 00:34:58,636
was defined so that it was a
limit of these guys, fn of x.

374
00:34:58,636 --> 00:35:04,290
So now we need to promote this
convergence that was pointwise,

375
00:35:04,290 --> 00:35:06,930
and we need to promote
the convergence

376
00:35:06,930 --> 00:35:08,830
to uniform convergence.

377
00:35:11,370 --> 00:35:12,590
So now we need--

378
00:35:18,650 --> 00:35:22,970
so given epsilon
greater than 0, again,

379
00:35:22,970 --> 00:35:27,410
since these guys,
fn, was a Cauchy

380
00:35:27,410 --> 00:35:38,130
sequence in the metric space--

381
00:35:38,130 --> 00:35:43,410
in this metric space--

382
00:35:43,410 --> 00:35:52,650
then as we saw over there,
there exists capital N

383
00:35:52,650 --> 00:35:59,090
so that if little n and little m
are bigger than this capital N,

384
00:35:59,090 --> 00:36:05,170
then the difference
between these two, fn

385
00:36:05,170 --> 00:36:15,680
of x minus fm of x, this thing
here, is less than or equal to--

386
00:36:15,680 --> 00:36:18,320
and I'm using epsilon over 2--

387
00:36:18,320 --> 00:36:19,380
for all x.

388
00:36:26,456 --> 00:36:30,120
So you have that.

389
00:36:30,120 --> 00:36:36,960
But now this here
holds for all m and n.

390
00:36:36,960 --> 00:36:39,960
Let's just fix n.

391
00:36:39,960 --> 00:36:41,800
And now we can use that.

392
00:36:41,800 --> 00:36:43,360
It's still true.

393
00:36:43,360 --> 00:36:45,720
I can still let m vary.

394
00:36:45,720 --> 00:36:56,400
And so if you let m go-- so
fixed n bigger than capital N.

395
00:36:56,400 --> 00:37:04,870
And let little m go to infinity.

396
00:37:04,870 --> 00:37:09,590
Well, this has just
some fixed number.

397
00:37:09,590 --> 00:37:14,430
But as m goes to infinity,
this here goes to f of x.

398
00:37:14,430 --> 00:37:17,510
And so since this difference
is always less than or equal

399
00:37:17,510 --> 00:37:22,630
to epsilon over 2,
it's also the case

400
00:37:22,630 --> 00:37:29,870
that the difference
here of the limit

401
00:37:29,870 --> 00:37:33,930
is less than or equal
to epsilon over 2.

402
00:37:33,930 --> 00:37:36,330
So you have this here for all x.

403
00:37:41,110 --> 00:37:48,270
This is just letting m
go through to the limit.

404
00:37:48,270 --> 00:37:54,790
But this, of course, says
that the max, if you will--

405
00:37:54,790 --> 00:37:55,770
so you have that.

406
00:37:55,770 --> 00:38:01,270
So this implies
that the max of fn

407
00:38:01,270 --> 00:38:07,190
of x minus f of x,
that this thing here,

408
00:38:07,190 --> 00:38:12,390
where x is now in this interval,
that this is less than or equal

409
00:38:12,390 --> 00:38:12,920
to epsilon.

410
00:38:15,942 --> 00:38:17,670
But this is the same.

411
00:38:17,670 --> 00:38:24,210
This here is just the
distance between fn and f.

412
00:38:24,210 --> 00:38:28,790
So say that this distance
here is less than--

413
00:38:28,790 --> 00:38:31,210
I guess I had epsilon over 2.

414
00:38:31,210 --> 00:38:36,990
So it means that this thing here
is strictly less than epsilon.

415
00:38:36,990 --> 00:38:39,290
It's actually less than or
equal to epsilon over 2.

416
00:38:39,290 --> 00:38:41,870
So it's strictly
less than epsilon.

417
00:38:41,870 --> 00:38:45,810
But that's exactly what it
means for this sequence here.

418
00:38:45,810 --> 00:38:48,350
So this is the same.

419
00:38:48,350 --> 00:39:01,650
This is the same as saying that
fn converges to f uniformly.

420
00:39:10,040 --> 00:39:13,180
Sorry, here, i should
have taken the sup.

421
00:39:13,180 --> 00:39:16,380
The reason why I
should take the sup

422
00:39:16,380 --> 00:39:21,200
here is that f is
just a function.

423
00:39:21,200 --> 00:39:24,600
It's not necessarily
continuous at this stage.

424
00:39:24,600 --> 00:39:27,420
But it was clearly
the case with the sup.

425
00:39:27,420 --> 00:39:30,940
So the maximum a priori
may not be achieved yet.

426
00:39:30,940 --> 00:39:34,340
But now you see
that they converge.

427
00:39:34,340 --> 00:39:39,460
These fn converge
to f uniformly.

428
00:39:39,460 --> 00:39:42,760
And these guys here
was continuous.

429
00:39:42,760 --> 00:39:45,940
These are continuous.

430
00:39:45,940 --> 00:39:49,060
So this implies
that by this theorem

431
00:39:49,060 --> 00:39:54,700
we just proved, that
f is also continuous.

432
00:39:54,700 --> 00:39:57,060
And so you see that--
so now you have

433
00:39:57,060 --> 00:40:00,520
that the limit is
indeed in the space.

434
00:40:00,520 --> 00:40:06,180
And we already proved here that
the convergence to this limit

435
00:40:06,180 --> 00:40:11,600
is uniform, which means
that it's in the metric.

436
00:40:15,375 --> 00:40:18,840
The next thing is that--

437
00:40:18,840 --> 00:40:22,660
so this here was just a
little bit different way

438
00:40:22,660 --> 00:40:24,660
of thinking about
uniform convergence,

439
00:40:24,660 --> 00:40:28,780
that you think about it more
abstractly as a convergence

440
00:40:28,780 --> 00:40:30,200
in a metric space.

441
00:40:30,200 --> 00:40:33,020
And it's a useful way
of thinking about it.

442
00:40:33,020 --> 00:40:34,700
The next thing we
want to look at

443
00:40:34,700 --> 00:40:41,740
is how this uniform
convergence gives you

444
00:40:41,740 --> 00:40:44,640
a way of computing things.

445
00:40:44,640 --> 00:40:50,020
So in particular, if you
have some power series.

446
00:40:50,020 --> 00:40:54,340
And we talked about that
a little bit last time.

447
00:40:54,340 --> 00:40:56,580
We didn't prove anything.

448
00:40:56,580 --> 00:41:00,180
But one of the
motivation for this

449
00:41:00,180 --> 00:41:03,570
is that suppose you take
a power series, like this.

450
00:41:08,220 --> 00:41:11,050
And we'll return to
this in just a second.

451
00:41:11,050 --> 00:41:13,930
But suppose you take a
power series, like this,

452
00:41:13,930 --> 00:41:17,470
and you are inside the
radius of convergence.

453
00:41:17,470 --> 00:41:21,790
So inside radius of convergence.

454
00:41:26,850 --> 00:41:29,250
So you know that this
thing here makes sense,

455
00:41:29,250 --> 00:41:33,410
that this, for each
x, gives you a number.

456
00:41:33,410 --> 00:41:35,070
And so it gives you a function.

457
00:41:35,070 --> 00:41:37,450
And so you're
interested in, what

458
00:41:37,450 --> 00:41:39,770
is the derivative
of that function?

459
00:41:39,770 --> 00:41:43,470
And what is the integral
of that function?

460
00:41:43,470 --> 00:41:46,730
How do we integrate it, and
how do we take the derivative?

461
00:41:46,730 --> 00:41:48,530
And the thing that
worries you a bit

462
00:41:48,530 --> 00:41:52,750
is that if it was just a finite
sum, it would be trivial.

463
00:41:52,750 --> 00:41:54,850
But it's an infinite sum.

464
00:41:54,850 --> 00:41:57,070
And so is it OK--

465
00:41:57,070 --> 00:42:00,330
so the natural
question is, is it OK?

466
00:42:00,330 --> 00:42:02,760
If you want to take
the derivative,

467
00:42:02,760 --> 00:42:06,960
can you interchange
this infinite sum

468
00:42:06,960 --> 00:42:08,920
with taking derivative?

469
00:42:08,920 --> 00:42:12,920
And likewise, if you want to
integrate this infinite sum,

470
00:42:12,920 --> 00:42:18,640
is it OK to interchange
the infinite sum taking

471
00:42:18,640 --> 00:42:19,920
the integral?

472
00:42:19,920 --> 00:42:23,840
And the answer is, it's OK.

473
00:42:23,840 --> 00:42:25,720
So let's see that.

474
00:42:25,720 --> 00:42:27,960
So we do this.

475
00:42:27,960 --> 00:42:29,880
So we have two
versions of this, one

476
00:42:29,880 --> 00:42:32,680
that's dealing with
taking integrals

477
00:42:32,680 --> 00:42:36,060
and another version that
deals with taking derivatives.

478
00:42:45,300 --> 00:42:49,440
And so the first
thing is that I take--

479
00:42:49,440 --> 00:42:52,960
so the first theorem
is the following,

480
00:42:52,960 --> 00:42:56,860
that I have some
interval from a to b.

481
00:43:02,200 --> 00:43:08,000
And then I have a
sequence of functions, fn,

482
00:43:08,000 --> 00:43:13,000
that are integrable.

483
00:43:13,000 --> 00:43:17,920
They may not be continuous,
they're just integrable.

484
00:43:17,920 --> 00:43:23,320
So in particular,
they are bounded.

485
00:43:23,320 --> 00:43:32,380
And then I have that fn here
converges to f uniformly.

486
00:43:37,720 --> 00:43:41,060
And I want to prove that--

487
00:43:41,060 --> 00:43:52,220
so the claim is that
fn is also integrable.

488
00:43:55,280 --> 00:44:07,210
And the integral of f is equal
to the limit of these integrals.

489
00:44:20,950 --> 00:44:24,390
Sorry, in particular, of
course, those integrals

490
00:44:24,390 --> 00:44:26,230
here has a limit.

491
00:44:26,230 --> 00:44:27,530
It's part of the statement.

492
00:44:51,690 --> 00:44:52,190
OK.

493
00:44:58,060 --> 00:44:58,560
Right.

494
00:45:02,540 --> 00:45:05,710
So what do we want to--
what do we need to prove?

495
00:45:05,710 --> 00:45:06,210
So prove.

496
00:45:10,280 --> 00:45:12,220
So we want to show--

497
00:45:12,220 --> 00:45:21,200
so we first want to show
that f is integrable.

498
00:45:26,420 --> 00:45:28,900
And we had our criteria
for doing that.

